N2  - With the rise of green propulsion systems alongside the growing need for an easier access to space by private aerospace companies and small satellite research groups, hybrid rocket engines start representing an attractive option for in-space propulsion because of their inherent advantages, such as safety, low toxicity, and low cost. However, challenges emerge in case of miniaturized engines requiring long-duration firings and/or multiple ignitions (as foreseen in different small satellite applications), since ablative passive thermal protection systems typically employed in the nozzle suffer excessive throat erosion and general thermal management issues. To address this, the present study proposes an efficient numerical approach for designing and analyze active regenerative cooling systems for small satellite hybrid propulsion, using carbon-based nozzles and liquid nitrous oxide (i.e., N2O) as coolant. The mathematical model proposed is firstly validated using data of an actively-cooled hybrid engine prototype recently tested at the Hokkaido University. The phase-change behavior of N2O is looked in details, and an evaporation rate law is proposed based on an ad-hoc reconstruction technique. Then, a simplified cooling system scalability study is carried out, underlining the potential advantages of a graphite-based liner over classical metal-based cooling jackets configurations. Lastly, an optimization algorithm for parametric design and optimization of the cooling system is introduced. The design algorithm is used to analyze two thrust-classes (≈100N and ≈1N) representative of hybrid engines in development at Letara Ltd., revealing that, while designing efficient cooling systems becomes more challenging with smaller engine scales, both engine scales can be successfully cooled using regenerative cooling methods, even considering manufacturing constraints related to 3D printing limits.
AB  - With the rise of green propulsion systems alongside the growing need for an easier access to space by private aerospace companies and small satellite research groups, hybrid rocket engines start representing an attractive option for in-space propulsion because of their inherent advantages, such as safety, low toxicity, and low cost. However, challenges emerge in case of miniaturized engines requiring long-duration firings and/or multiple ignitions (as foreseen in different small satellite applications), since ablative passive thermal protection systems typically employed in the nozzle suffer excessive throat erosion and general thermal management issues. To address this, the present study proposes an efficient numerical approach for designing and analyze active regenerative cooling systems for small satellite hybrid propulsion, using carbon-based nozzles and liquid nitrous oxide (i.e., N2O) as coolant. The mathematical model proposed is firstly validated using data of an actively-cooled hybrid engine prototype recently tested at the Hokkaido University. The phase-change behavior of N2O is looked in details, and an evaporation rate law is proposed based on an ad-hoc reconstruction technique. Then, a simplified cooling system scalability study is carried out, underlining the potential advantages of a graphite-based liner over classical metal-based cooling jackets configurations. Lastly, an optimization algorithm for parametric design and optimization of the cooling system is introduced. The design algorithm is used to analyze two thrust-classes (≈100N and ≈1N) representative of hybrid engines in development at Letara Ltd., revealing that, while designing efficient cooling systems becomes more challenging with smaller engine scales, both engine scales can be successfully cooled using regenerative cooling methods, even considering manufacturing constraints related to 3D printing limits.
